                <pb facs="0394" n="366" xml:id="pg366"/>

                <head>155. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>In a February 15, 1962, letter to Chairman Khrushchev, President Kennedy wrote
                    that he had concluded that negotiations on Berlin would be protracted but
                    believed that, over the longer term, agreement might still be reached. In the
                    meantime, he argued, neither side should undertake any step which could lead to
                    an increase in tensions over Berlin. The President also mentioned the
                    possibility of creating an international commission with East German
                    participation which would supervise access to Berlin. For text, see <ref
                        target="frus:frus1961-63v06/pg_92"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations</hi>, 1961–1963, volume VI, pages 92</ref>–<ref
                        target="frus:frus1961-63v06/pg_95">95</ref>.</p>
            </div>
